 `@1000` is a per-move wall-clock budget in milliseconds (default 4000; maximum 120000). `mcts/8@1000` runs eight independent search trees in goroutines and merges root visits. `ab/8@1000` distributes alpha-beta root moves across eight workers; `par-ab` selects up to eight workers. Each depth must complete all root moves before replacing the previous result, and ties follow serial move order. The depth limit remains four. `-jobs` runs multiple matches in goroutines. `-reuse=false` disables MCTS subtree retention across turns. All bots search the full server state, including actual deck and bag order, shuffle RNG, and both players' blind reservations. Parallel MCTS workers share that starting position and use separate rollout randomness. Alpha-beta uses iterative deepening through depth four; MCTS uses UCT and greedy rollouts. Alpha-beta uses the server’s immutable Go rule engine. MCTS uses a compact simulation kernel with array-based state, encoded moves, and direct heuristic scoring; seeded differential tests check its generated moves and transitions against the authoritative engine. MCTS moves in session JSONL records include `search: {simulations, moveSimulations, winRate}` for later analysis.

-MCTS disables voluntary replenishment by default. Enable it with `mcts/8+refill@1000` in the arena or `"includeRefill": true` in the API agent config. The flag applies to both tree search and rollouts; forced replenishment remains available regardless. Enabled replenishment uses the actual bag order and shuffle state, grants the opponent a privilege, and can precede a privilege-assisted purchase. The chosen move records replenishment explicitly, so replay and parallel root statistics preserve that choice.
+MCTS searches voluntary replenishment by default only when the board has at most three non-gold tokens and no card can be bought, including reserved cards and privilege-assisted purchases. Forced replenishment remains available regardless. Both tree search and rollouts exclude smaller token takes whenever a larger legal take exists on the same board. Replenishment uses the actual bag order and shuffle state, grants the opponent a privilege, and can precede a privilege-assisted purchase. The chosen move records replenishment explicitly, so replay and parallel root statistics preserve that choice. Legacy `+refill` and `"includeRefill"` options remain accepted; MCTS always enables this restricted policy.

 The browser still offers human play, step/auto agent play, and saved-session resume. Only its transport adapter and the removed agent controls changed. Old `ab`, `mcts2`, and `par-mcts2` specs are compatibility aliases for alpha-beta, MCTS, and parallel MCTS respectively. The Go MCTS ports the TypeScript compact-kernel approach with stateless tree nodes: every simulation replays its path from the root on one scratch state, so node memory no longer scales with position size. HTTP bot turns and arena matches retain per-seat subtrees across turns by default (`search.Session`; `-reuse=false` in the arena); any tree whose full state or refill policy no longer matches the live game is discarded. Historical strength measurements apply to the TypeScript agents. Run `go test ./internal/search -run '^$' -bench 'Benchmark(Rollout|MCTS)$' -benchmem` to measure Go rollout cost and search throughput on the saved single-token position.

